3. Ensuring proper grounding and bonding.  

As for telecommunications, whether it’s a data center, server room, or the building’s communication lines, there is a designated space (often referred to as a “space”) for swapping or installing equipment. This space is typically led by the electrical discipline but is actually managed by the low-voltage (weak current) discipline, serving as the equivalent of the electrical room for the high-voltage (strong current) discipline.  

Generally, tasks like wiring jumpers for high-voltage or low-voltage systems, as well as final testing of IT computers—including network setup, topology, or software—are handled by the IT team, not us. These are the standards used for telecommunications design, with the U.S. national standard being TIA/EIA Big C.

It can be roughly divided into three types based on the system’s functions:  
1. Access Control System, called Access Control;  
2. Camera, also known as Video Surveillance, which is for monitoring;  
3. Intrusion Detection System, also called an alarm system, but it’s not a fire alarm system—it’s for burglary prevention.  

These three systems can be used individually, in pairs, or all together. They require system integration. Of course, such systems involve both software and hardware, as well as programming, debugging, etc.  

Ultimately, there will be a Security Monitoring system, which is essentially the same as the Far One Monitoring I mentioned earlier. In North America, there are third-party companies specialized in monitoring fire alarms and burglary alarms. These companies operate 24/7, with staff on duty who are typically security-level personnel trained for such tasks. In the event of an alarm, they can usually arrive within five to ten minutes—very quickly.
